.TH WINCAN 1 "November 2026" "wincan" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wincan \- a small frameless candle chart window for Windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wincan.exe
[\fB\-\-server\fR [\fBhttps://\fR]\fIHOST:PORT\fR] [\fB\-\-pair\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-tf\fR \fIFRAME\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wincan
shows the candles and last price of one coin, from a
.BR candled (8)
server, in a 300 by 175 pixel window with no frame, border or title bar that stays on top of other windows.  It
refreshes once a second.  If a position (an entry price and a size) is entered, it shows the profit or loss as a percentage and
in dollars, green when ahead and red when behind, and draws the entry price as a dotted line.
.SS Keyboard and focus
The window takes the keyboard when the mouse comes over it, with no click (nothing is drawn around it to show that it has the keyboard).  Move the mouse out of the window and it gives the keyboard back at once to the window that had it (except while an entry or
size is being typed).  If the keyboard is given up with the mouse still over the window (Esc, or another window taken with Alt+Tab), the window does not take it back until the mouse has left and come again, or it is clicked.
.TP
.B Up, Down
the timeframe: 1m 3m 5m 15m 30m 1h 4h 1d 1w.
.TP
.B Left, Right
the coin, in the order the server lists them.
.TP
.B e
open the position editor, a panel with four boxes: the entry price, the amount in coins (negative for a short), the leverage and the realized profit or loss in dollars (what has already been taken on the position).  It opens on the box last edited, and can be opened again at any time until the trade is closed with x.  Tab, or Up and Down, go between the boxes, Enter, or moving the mouse out of the window, says the position has been entered (done); Esc cancels.  A box left empty keeps what it held; the first key typed in a box replaces its contents.  The Left and Right arrow keys, Home and End move a caret inside the box, Delete and Backspace work at the caret and a key typed goes in at it, so that a minus sign can be put in front of a number without typing the number again; the first of these keys keeps the contents, where the first ordinary key replaces them.
.TP
.B g
open the go-to box: type a ticker and the pairs that match are listed below it (an exact match first, then those that begin with what has been typed, then those that contain it, at most five), with the rest of the chosen one shown dimmed after the letters.  Tab completes the text with the chosen pair, Up and Down choose another, Enter goes straight to it, and Esc, or moving the mouse out of the window, cancels.  The Left and Right arrow keys, Home, End, Delete and Backspace edit the text at a caret.
.TP
.B Delete
clear the position of this coin.
.TP
.B t
hide the window in the tray.
.TP
.B f
toggle full screen: the window covers the whole monitor it is on and f again puts it back.  The window's place and size are remembered, and whatever else ends full screen (Esc, another window taking the keyboard, hiding to the tray, quitting, a change of the display) puts it back at the same place and size.  The chart fills the screen with its text at the full screen magnification (see \fBfullscale\fR in wincan(5)); no more than 295 candles are shown (the server sends 500 and the 200\-period average needs 200 of them), so on a wide screen they are wider than in the small window.  The window cannot be dragged in full screen, and it is never in full screen when started.
.TP
.B + , \-
make the window larger or smaller (75% to 400%); in full screen, the magnification of the text (the window itself keeps its size for when it comes back).
.TP
.B q
quit the program.
.TP
.B x
close the trade of the coin on show at the current price (the mark) and log it, see TRADE LOG.
.TP
.B Esc
leave full screen; otherwise (and first, to close the editor or the go\-to box if one is open) give the keyboard back.
The line across the window at the latest price is green when the candle forming is up and red when it is down.  The highest high and the lowest low of the bars on show are each marked by a dotted line across the window with its value at the left.
A dashed horizontal line follows the mouse up and down the chart, in the small window and in full screen, and the price under it is shown at the top, in the middle; within the range of the candles the pointer snaps to the nearest one (standard and volume candles alike): coming to a candle it snaps sideways to the middle of that candle and is held there until your real motion would have taken it out of the candle, when it snaps to the next one (the pointer's own movement is summed to know this), the candle is drawn brighter with a soft halo, and its date and time (local time; the date only for day and week candles; the year too in full screen) are shown beside the price, with no vertical line.  Vertically the pointer locks onto the open, high, low and close of that candle and onto the Bollinger and moving\-average lines there (those that are on), within a few pixels (4 at 100%): it is held on the line in the same way, and let go when your real motion would have taken it out of those few pixels; the price shown is then the line's exact value.  Otherwise (the mouse off the candles or off the chart) the current local date and time, with seconds, are shown there instead: one or the other, never both; where the row is too tight (the small window with the price on show) the clock is the time only.
.SS Indicators
Small dots at the bottom right switch the Bollinger bands (20 periods, 2 standard deviations; violet), the 9\-period moving average (orange) and the 200\-period moving average (blue).  A filled dot is on, a ring is off; hover over a dot for its name, click it to switch it.  They are kept in the settings.  A fourth, white dot switches the volume candles, drawn as KuCoin's chart draws them (the rule was measured from two of its screenshots against the real volumes, see volume-candles.txt in the sources): no candle is thinner than a standard one; the width starts to grow at the mean volume on show and grows with the logarithm of the volume, as max(1, 1.10 + 1.57 ln(volume/mean)) times the standard width, in an odd number of pixels; the candles do not overlap: each keeps its own width, side by side, so the wide ones take room and fewer candles are on show.  The standard candles are all as wide as each other.
.SS Moving
Click and drag anywhere to move the window.  Nothing keeps it on the screen, it may be dragged partly off, but whenever an edge of the window comes within 11 pixels (as the program sees them: Windows scales them with the screen, so on a screen scaled to 150% that is about 17 pixels on the glass) of an edge of the usable screen (the taskbar's edge counts), from inside or from outside, the two snap together: the window sits flush inside the edge, or flush just outside it; and it lets go again when taken further than that.
.SS Tray and Alt-Tab
A tray icon shows or hides the window with a click; its menu has Exit.  Ctrl+Alt+C does the same from anywhere.  While shown the
window is in Alt\-Tab and has a taskbar button, which minimizes it.  A window hidden in the tray is not in Alt\-Tab: use the icon
or Ctrl+Alt+C.
.SH FIGURES
As on KuCoin's position panel: the large figures are the unrealized profit or loss, in dollars and as a return on the initial margin (the entry value, entry times amount, divided by the leverage; the whole value if no leverage is given); the realized amount and the net (unrealized plus realized) are shown apart under them.  The price used is the mark price, as on KuCoin (which values a position at its mark price, recomputed every second, not at the last trade, so the figures move every second even when nobody trades); the server (candled with --official-klines) fetches it for the pair being watched, and without it the last trade is used.  A trade closed with x is closed at the mark.
.SH TRADE LOG
When a trade is closed with
.BR x ,
one line is appended to
.B %APPDATA%\\wincan\\trades.csv
(a header line is written first): the time closed, the coin, long or short, the leverage, the entry and the size, the time opened, the exit price, the unrealized profit or loss, the realized one, their total, the margin (the value of the position divided by the leverage, or the whole value if none was given), the return on the margin in per cent, the highest and lowest price since entry with the time of each, the best and the worst the trade went through from the entry (the maximum favorable and maximum adverse excursion, in per cent of the margin and in dollars) and the duration in seconds.  The highest and lowest prices are asked of the server, so they are right even if the window was not running meanwhile.  While a position is open the window shows the best and worst so far.
.SH ONE COPY
Only one copy runs: starting a second finds the first, shows its window (it may be hidden in the tray) and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-server " \fR[\fBhttps://\fR]\fIHOST:PORT"
the candle supply (also kept in the settings file).  With https:// the connection is encrypted and authenticated by a key of this computer's own, which the server must have authorized, and the server's certificate is pinned (see wincan(5): client_cert, server_pin).
.TP
.B \-\-fingerprint
print the SHA\-256 fingerprint of this computer's key, for \fBcandled \-\-authorize\fR, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
display help or version information and exit (on the console that started wincan, else in a message box).
.TP
.BI \-\-pair " NAME" ", " \-\-tf " FRAME"
start with this coin and timeframe.
.TP
.BI \-\-shot " FILE.bmp"
draw once and write the picture to a file, then exit (for testing; with \fB\-\-shot\-position\fR \fIENTRY,SIZE\fR and \fB\-\-shot\-active\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-shot\-full " WIDTHxHEIGHT"
with \fB\-\-shot\fR, draw the full screen layout for a monitor of that size.
.TP
.BI \-\-shot\-mouse " Y"
draw the line that follows the mouse as if the mouse were at that height, and with \fB\-\-shot\-mousex\fR \fIX\fR at that distance from the left.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B %APPDATA%\\wincan\\wincan.ini
settings; see
.BR wincan (5).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR candled (8),
.BR wincan (5)
.SH HEAT STRIP
The last 25 pixels of the window show the modelled liquidations of the pair: left of the centre, longs liquidated as the price falls (green bars, marked L); right, shorts liquidated as it rises (red bars, marked S);
the centre line is the present price.  One bar per percentage point from the price, twenty a side, the nearest next to the centre.  A bar's height is the
square root of its liquidation notional (the biggest bar of the pair fills the strip); its brightness is the pressure on the order book.  The white ball is the centre of gravity as a plain mass-weighted average, the gold ring
the Newtonian one (each cluster weighted by mass over distance squared); each sits at its per cent from the price on the strip's linear axis (20 per cent at the edge).
With the mouse over a bar (anywhere in its column of the strip) the bar is shown brighter on a soft haze and the top centre shows its side, its distance from the present price in per cent (bar i is i to i+1 per cent: L -5 to -6%, S +8 to +9%, the nearest 0 to -1% or 0 to +1%) and the price range that is, from the nearest edge to the farthest; where the row is too narrow, the middle of the bar only.
The data come from the server's /heat (see candled(8)).
The cyan ball is the net skew, (shorts \- longs) / (shorts + longs) of the whole ladder, placed across the half strip: at an edge when one side has everything to lose, in the centre when the two sides are even.
It answers who has more to lose, which the two centres of gravity, the mean of two piles on either side of the price, mostly cancel.
